     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\">I&#39;m getting unexpected behavior with syntax with a custom lpdf function. If the issue is there&#39;s an actual bug, I&#39;ll create a MWE and submit it on Github. If it&#39;s just that I misunderstand the syntax.<div><br></div><div>Consider two functions:</div><div><br></div><blockquote style=\"margin:0 0 0 40px;border:none;padding:0px\"><div><div>real lower_truncated_normal_lpdf(real x, real mu, real sigma, real A) {</div></div><div><div>    return(normal_lpdf(x | mu, sigma) - normal_lccdf(A | mu, sigma));</div></div><div><div>} // This function isn&#39;t always necessary, since sometimes we can use Stan&#39;s built-in syntax of normal(x|mu, sigma) T[A,], but sometimes it is still needed</div></div><div><div><br></div></div><div><div>real lower_truncated_normal_lpdf_vector(vector x, real mu, real sigma, real A) {</div></div><div><div>    return(normal_lpdf(x | mu, sigma) - normal_lccdf(A | mu, sigma));</div></div><div><div>} // As the first function, but assume the input is a vector.</div></div></blockquote><div><br></div><div>The issue is in calling them:</div><div><br></div><blockquote style=\"margin:0 0 0 40px;border:none;padding:0px\"><div><span style=\"color:rgb(38,50,56);font-family:arial,sans-serif\">lower_truncated_normal_lpdf(x[t] | mu, sigma, 0) //x[t] is a scalar. This runs.</span></div><div><br style=\"color:rgb(38,50,56);font-family:arial,sans-serif\"></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(38,50,56);font-family:arial,sans-serif\">lower_truncated_normal_lpdf_vector(x | mu, sigma, 0) //x is a vector. This fails with a parser error. </span></div><div><br style=\"color:rgb(38,50,56);font-family:arial,sans-serif\"></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(38,50,56);font-family:arial,sans-serif\">lower_truncated_normal_lpdf_vector(x, mu, sigma, 0) //x is a vector. This runs.</span></div></blockquote><div><span style=\"color:rgb(38,50,56);font-family:arial,sans-serif\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"color:rgb(38,50,56);font-family:arial,sans-serif\">I can understand why the second one fails but the third runs. But what I don&#39;t understand is why the first then runs.</span></div><div><br></div><div>Here&#39;s the exact parser error:</div><blockquote style=\"margin:0 0 0 40px;border:none;padding:0px\"><div><pre style=\"box-sizing:border-box;overflow:auto;font-size:14px;line-height:inherit;word-break:break-all;word-wrap:break-word;color:rgb(0,0,0);border-radius:0px;white-space:pre-wrap;vertical-align:baseline\"><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:steelblue\"><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:darkgreen\"><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:steelblue\"><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:darkgreen\"><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:steelblue\"><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:darkgreen\"><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:steelblue\"><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:darkgreen\"><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:steelblue\"><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:darkgreen\"><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:darkred\">target += lower_truncated_normal_lpdf_vector(x | mu, sigma, 0);</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></pre></div><div><pre style=\"box-sizing:border-box;overflow:auto;font-size:14px;line-height:inherit;word-break:break-all;word-wrap:break-word;color:rgb(0,0,0);border-radius:0px;white-space:pre-wrap;vertical-align:baseline\"><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:steelblue\"><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:darkgreen\"><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:steelblue\"><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:darkgreen\"><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:steelblue\"><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:darkgreen\"><span style=\"box-sizing:border-box;color:steelblue\"><span style=\"box-sizi